/**
 * Internal reviewer notes for ./corporate-practice-of-medicine: the reasoning,
 * exceptions, conflicting sources, and recent law changes behind each state's
 * status. Never rendered, and never imported by UI code (the public data
 * module ships to the browser). Update a state's note when it is re-reviewed.
 */
export const CORPORATE_PRACTICE_REVIEW_NOTES: Record<string, string> = {
  AL: "Verified PDF text: Oct 28, 1992 MLC ruling (adopting BME ruling of Oct 21, 1992) holds a general business corporation employing a licensed physician is not engaged in unlicensed practice (34-24-51) and the physician is not aiding/abetting (34-24-360(13)) or fee splitting, provided the employment agreement gives the physician final authority over all medical decisions. Also reviewed the BME IV therapy declaratory ruling (albme.gov/uploads/pdfs/IV_Therapy.pdf, investigation 2021-2022, published ~2023): a business 'can own and operate' a retail IV therapy business, but anyone maintaining an office to diagnose/treat/prescribe IV medication is engaged in unlawful practice unless it (1) employs a physician (or physician plus PA/CRNP/CNM under registration/collaboration) and (2) that clinician has exclusive authority over diagnosis and prescribing. So 'paper' medical directors issuing standing orders without real involvement are a compliance risk in AL. The ruling uses the word 'employs'; reviewer may want to check whether independent-contractor Medical Director arrangements are treated the same. Aggregators also cite Ala. Att'y Gen. Op. 2001-089 (not independently verified). No 2023-2026 CPOM legislation found.",
  AK: "No primary source found that affirmatively addresses CPOM in Alaska, which is itself consistent with no doctrine. Source is a law-firm-prepared 50-state chart (author firm not identified; PDF metadata shows Jan 2023) filed as testimony by the ND Medical Association; it lists Alaska as 'No' CPOM, citing only AS 08.64.170. MIEC (malpractice insurer) FAQ on medispas (2013, https://www.miec.com/knowledge-library/faq-medispas/) likewise says 'Alaska state laws do not speak to the corporate practice of medicine.' Professional corporations under AS 10.45 must be owned by licensees, but that only applies if the owner chooses a PC; a general business corporation or LLC is not barred. No 2023-2026 CPOM or PE legislation found.",
  AZ: "Verified Midtown text: an outpatient treatment center licensed by ADHS and wholly owned by a non-licensed engineer could employ physicians and chiropractors; court said the outpatient treatment center statutes/regulations 'expressly permit general corporate ownership', but expressly limited the ruling to that 'narrow issue'. Funk Jewelry (1935, optometry) is the older case recognizing a corporate practice bar. No AZ statute prohibits lay ownership and the Arizona Medical Board regulates licensees, not entity ownership. NPs have full practice authority in AZ (can own and serve as their own clinical lead). Borderline between EXCEPTIONS and PERMISSIVE; I chose EXCEPTIONS because the only modern holding is tied to ADHS-licensed facilities. Reviewer may reasonably choose PERMISSIVE. No 2023-2026 CPOM legislation found.",
  AR: "Source is an Arkansas Dept. of Health / State Board of Nursing overview dated 12/2025 (file name 1.15.26). It says the Medical Corporation Act limits ownership, management and control of medical corporations and medical PLLCs to persons licensed under the Medical Practices Act, BUT that 'interpretation and enforcement appear inconsistent across professional boards' and many clinics are APRN-owned in practice ('de facto acceptance'). WebFetch could not resolve the host (DNS error in the fetch tool); the PDF downloaded fine via curl (HTTP 200) and the text was read in full. AG opinions: CourtListener confirms a 1994-08-17 AG opinion (to Sen. Vic Snyder) answering whether a non-physician-owned corporation may employ physicians for profit (law-firm surveys cite it as Op. 94-204 concluding it is not authorized, with nonprofit medical service corporations allowed to contract but not employ). I could not load the opinion body to verify its conclusion. Op. 2014-118 is cited by surveys but not verified. Status is arguable: EXCEPTIONS would also be defensible given the state's own admission of inconsistent enforcement. No 2023-2026 CPOM legislation found.",
  CA: "Verified MBC page lists as prohibited: non-physicians owning or operating a business that offers patient evaluation, diagnosis, care or treatment; physicians practicing through an LLC, LLP or general corporation; MSOs that arrange for or provide medical services rather than only administrative services; and physicians acting as 'medical director' without ownership. Old MBC URL /Licensees/Corporate_Practice.aspx now 404s. MBC 'The Business of Medicine: Medical Spas' PDF (https://www.mbc.ca.gov/Download/Documents/medical-spas-business.pdf) explicitly attacks 'rent-a-license' Medical Director programs and says the law does not allow nurses to set up a practice in a salon and hire a physician supervisor. Physicians must own at least 51% of a professional medical corporation; certain other licensees may hold minority shares. RECENT: SB 351 (signed Oct 6, 2025, effective Jan 1, 2026) codifies CPOM limits on private equity and hedge fund involvement and bars certain noncompete and nondisparagement clauses in their management contracts. AB 1415 (2025) extends Office of Health Care Affordability transaction notice and review to MSOs, PE groups and hedge funds. Licensed nonprofit charitable clinics that do not charge for professional services are a narrow exception (B&P 2400; 16 CCR 1340).",
  CO: "Verified text (current through Fall 2025): 12-240-138(6)(a) 'Corporations shall not practice medicine'; (6)(b) employment of a physician in accordance with 25-3-103.7 is not the corporate practice of medicine. 25-3-103.7 lets a 'health-care facility' employ physicians, defined as hospitals, hospices, behavioral health safety net providers (from July 1, 2024), FQHCs, school-based health centers, rural health clinics, PACE organizations and long-term care facilities. Med spas and general clinics are not on the list. Medical PCs/PLLCs must be physician-owned (PAs may hold a minority). Could be labeled EXCEPTIONS because of the facility carve-outs, but for the target audience (med spa, IV, wellness owners) none of the exceptions apply, so STRICT. RECENT: HB26-1249 (would have let RNs, APRNs, PAs, estheticians and cosmetologists own medical-aesthetics professional corporations) was postponed indefinitely 13-0 in House committee on Mar 25, 2026, so not law. SB25-198 (transaction notice) was introduced in 2025; I did not confirm whether it passed. SB25-083 (2025) restricted physician noncompetes.",
  CT: "CPOM basis: 1954 AG opinion reading the licensing statute (20-9) to restrict practice to individuals; Shipman & Goodwin (2010 CT Law Tribune reprint) confirms the doctrine is 'still very much alive' but has not been applied to nonprofit hospital employees, and PA 09-212 created medical foundations (33-182aa et seq.) as an exception. Med spa specifics: CGS 19a-903c (verified via FindLaw and the OLR report dated Sept 29, 2025) defines a medical spa as an establishment other than a hospital or licensed facility where cosmetic medical procedures are performed and requires it to 'employ or contract for the services of' a CT-licensed physician, PA or APRN with cosmetic training, who must do an initial in-person assessment. That statute does not require physician ownership, which supports lay-owned med spas, but general clinics/IV/wellness outside 19a-903c are still subject to the doctrine. WebFetch failed on cga.ct.gov due to a TLS certificate chain error in the fetch tool; the PDF was retrieved via curl and read. RECENT: PA 26-22 (signed May 27, 2026) bans hospital sale-leasebacks and requires hospital attestations about private equity control and interference with clinical judgment (hospital-focused, not med spas). 2025 PE bills (e.g., SB 1507, HB 6873) were introduced; not confirmed enacted.",
  DE: "No Delaware primary source found addressing CPOM. Law-firm 50-state chart (2023) lists Delaware as 'No' CPOM citing tit. 24 §§ 1701 et seq. Stevens & Lee blog (June 13, 2025, https://www.stevenslee.com/health-law-observer-blog/navigating-interstate-entity-issues-corporate-practice-of-medicine-and-ownership-considerations/) uses Delaware as an example of a state that 'may allow non-physician ownership subject to certain limitations'. Several AI-style aggregator sites claim Delaware bars lay ownership, but they appear to conflate PC shareholder rules with a CPOM ban. Delaware NPs have full practice authority. RECENT: SB 313 (temporary ban through July 1, 2028 on for-profit acquisition of nonprofit acute care hospitals) is hospital-focused, reported by secondary sources only, not verified.",
  DC: "Verified Policy 15-02 text: defines 'Medical Spa' as an entity 'owned by, or an organization of which the majority shares are held by, a physician(s) licensed in the District', requires Level II/III facilities to have a medical director, clinical director and site director (medical director on site at least 50% of operating hours), and says Level II/III procedures may be performed only as part of a medical practice. BUT the policy states it is 'directive in nature and not mandatory' until regulations are adopted (I did not find adopted regulations). Underlying CPOM law is unsettled: the 2023 law-firm survey lists DC as 'Yes' CPOM with no nonprofit exception (citing D.C. Code 3-1201.02(7)(A), 29-502, 29-503), while Group Health Ass'n (nonprofit using salaried physicians held not to be practicing medicine) cuts the other way. Group Health citation not independently verified. Classified EXCEPTIONS because the answer depends on structure. Reviewer should double check; STRICT is a plausible alternative for med spas specifically. No 2023-2026 changes found.",
  FL: "Florida has no CPOM statute, and Board of Medicine declaratory statements (from 1987/1989, e.g., Goulet, 89-COM-01) and Rush v. City of St. Petersburg (1967) recognize physicians may be employed by non-physician entities if the physician-patient relationship is not interfered with (confirmed by The Health Law Firm and Jones Health Law articles). Instead, the Health Care Clinic Act requires AHCA licensure for an entity that provides health care services and 'tenders charges for reimbursement', unless exempt; the key exemptions (400.9905(4)(f),(g)) are for entities wholly owned by licensed physicians/practitioners (or physician plus close family). Licensed clinics must appoint a medical director or clinic director who accepts legal responsibility for listed duties (400.9935, verified). Whether a cash-only med spa 'tenders charges for reimbursement' is an AHCA interpretation question; summary uses 'may need'. Unlicensed clinic operation carries serious penalties. Fee-splitting rules (Fla. Stat. 458.331(1)(i)) still matter for percentage-based management fees. RECENT: a Florida Medical Association 2023 resolution (23-305) on corporate practice of medicine exists; I could not load it to confirm its content. No 2023-2026 CPOM statute found.",
  GA: "Verified Health Horizons text: court says 'a business corporation cannot lawfully practice one of the so-called learned professions' but such a corporation is not void, a for-profit corporation may take assignment of physician fees for billing/collection without practicing medicine, and 'only the Composite State Board of Medical Examiners' has standing to act if a corporation is practicing medicine. Little Health Law Blog (Oct 25, 2022, Georgia health law firm, https://www.littlehealthlawblog.com/georgia-medical-spa-series-corporate-practice-of-medicine-doctrine/) says the standalone statute was repealed in 1982, CPOM principles are scattered in other code sections, the Georgia Composite Medical Board appears to have no issue with APRN ownership, and non-physicians can own med spas with a structure that prevents interference with clinical judgment. Secondary sources also report the Board said in 2011/2012 that it has never disciplined anyone for CPOM (not verified against minutes). Sherrer v. Hale (1982) also cited by surveys. Practically close to PERMISSIVE; labeled EXCEPTIONS because case law still states the doctrine and enforcement is just weak. No 2023-2026 CPOM legislation found.",
  HI: "No Hawaii primary source found on CPOM. 2023 law-firm survey lists Hawaii as 'No' CPOM citing HRS 453-2 and 448-15. Permit Health says the framework 'hints at a prohibition' but it has not become a significant issue. MIEC FAQ (2013) only notes that professional corporation shareholders must be licensed (HRS 415A), which does not bar a general corporation or LLC. Several med spa marketing sites (Portrait Care, Nextech) claim only physicians can own med spas in Hawaii, without citations; others say the opposite. Because of these conflicting secondary sources and no primary authority, confidence is LOW and EXCEPTIONS could be argued. RECENT: Hawaii SB 3175 (2026 healthcare transaction review) reportedly failed to advance; not verified.",
  ID: "Verified PDF text of the Board minutes: 'The Idaho State Board of Medicine hereby formally disavows and rejects the corporate practice of medicine doctrine' and 'will not discipline physicians or physician assistants solely because they practice medicine in association with or for unlicensed entities or persons.' The minutes are a primary Board document but are hosted on Holland & Hart's site, not a .gov site. Holland & Hart update (Mar 11, 2020) notes no case law or legislation since 2016 formally rescinds the doctrine for all purposes, so Worton v. Davis (1952) technically remains on the books, but the only enforcer has disavowed it. MIEC's 2013 FAQ describing an Idaho prohibition is outdated. Idaho's medical board now sits within the Division of Occupational and Professional Licenses (reorganized 2023); no indication the 2016 policy changed. No 2023-2026 CPOM legislation found.",
  IL: "Verified on FindLaw: Berlin reaffirms Kerner ('a corporation cannot employ physicians and collect fees for their services') and carves out licensed hospitals only. Carter-Shields v. Alton Health Inst., 201 Ill. 2d 441 (2002) refused to extend the hospital exception to a nonprofit health organization, so the exception is narrow. Medical Corporation Act / PLLC require physician owners; 225 ILCS 60/22 fee-splitting limits also constrain MSO fee structures (percentage fees are a known risk area). Recent: Illinois health care transaction notice law (740 ILCS 10/7.2a, eff. Jan 1, 2024) expanded by HB 5000 / Public Act 104-0782, signed Aug 7, 2026, eff. Jan 1, 2027: removes the sunset, broadens covered transactions to any transaction 'involving' health care entities, and expressly covers private equity companies including indirect ownership through MSOs/DSOs (30-day pre-closing AG notice, $500/day penalty). Verified via Reed Smith summary, not the ILGA page (ilga.gov refused connection).",
  IN: "Verified statute text: (c) says employment/contract between an entity in (a)(21)-(22) and a physician is not unlawful practice if the entity does not direct or control independent medical judgment. (a)(21) = licensed hospitals. (a)(22) = health care organizations whose members/shareholders/partners are licensed as physicians, hospitals, HMOs, health facilities, dentists, RNs/LPNs, nurse midwives, optometrists, podiatrists, chiropractors, PTs or psychologists. So an RN/NP-owned entity appears to qualify, but a purely lay-investor-owned med spa does not fit the statutory safe harbor. CPOM roots: Iterman v. Baker (1938). Federal cases (Orthodontic Affiliates v. OrthAlliance, 2002; Cohen v. OrthAlliance, 2003) read management agreements permissively. Secondary sources split between 'moderate' and 'limited' CPOM. Recent: IC 25-1-8.5 requires 90-day pre-closing AG notice for health care entity mergers/acquisitions ($10M asset threshold, includes private equity partnerships), eff. July 1, 2024; amended by HEA 1666 (2025) to exempt practices majority-owned by an Indiana-licensed practitioner who provides care there, add ownership reporting, and give the AG market-concentration investigative powers. Reviewer: confirm whether an RN/NP-owned med spa entity is treated as an (a)(22) organization in practice.",
  IA: 'Primary source not verified: the Iowa Board of Medicine page that republished AG Op. 91-7-1 (medicalboard.iowa.gov/document/corporate-practice-medicine) now redirects to a generic DIAL board page, and web.archive.org is not fetchable. Source used is a law-firm 50-state CPOM survey submitted as legislative testimony and hosted on ndlegis.gov (secondary). Per that survey: the AG opinion says violations turn on a case-by-case evaluation of control and dominion, and unless barred by statute or public policy against lay control, non-physician corporations may provide medical services through employed physicians. It rejects deciding the question on for-profit vs nonprofit status alone. Other secondary sites (Permit Health, Zivian) describe Iowa as a CPOM state, so classification is between EXCEPTIONS and PERMISSIVE. Conservative practitioners still often recommend a physician-owned PC plus MSO for med spas. Recent changes: none found for 2023 to 2026 (search was limited).',
  KS: "Verified syllabus: 'A general corporation is prohibited from providing medical services or acting through licensed practitioners; therefore, there can be no contract between the general corporation and third parties to perform medical services.' Justia copy (law.justia.com/cases/kansas/supreme-court/1991/65328-2.html) returned 403 to the fetcher, so vLex was used. St. Francis v. Weiss (1994) allows licensed (nonprofit) hospitals to employ physicians. K.S.A. 65-28,134 (verified on ksrevisor.gov) lets narrow 'business entities' with a Board certificate of authorization employ physicians: employer on-site clinics serving only their own employees/dependents, certain nonprofit/mutual health carriers serving enrollees, and certain EMR IT companies serving their own employees. None of these fit a med spa or IV clinic. The Kansas Board of Healing Arts has a 'Corporations' FAQ (ksbha.ks.gov/departments/general-counsel/general-counsel-faqs/corporations) but it was blocked (403) so not verified. Recent changes: none found.",
  KY: "No primary source verified online. KBML Opinion No. 36 (1995) is not posted where it could be found. Survey (secondary, law-firm table on ndlegis.gov) says Kentucky has a CPOM doctrine (KRS 311.560), charitable health care providers registered under KRS 216.940 are exempt, and the Kentucky medical board has indicated it will not enforce CPOM where the employer does not interfere with independent medical judgment. Other secondary sources describe a Kentucky AG opinion allowing corporate employment absent interference and say KBML has stated a for-profit corporation may hire a physician, while Kendall v. Beiling (1943) has not been overruled. Several surveys label Kentucky 'unsettled'. Med spa sites commonly recommend PC plus MSO but describe Kentucky as relatively permissive. Classification could arguably be PERMISSIVE. Recent changes: none found.",
  LA: "Primary source verified (PDF linked from the official LSBME 'Statements of Position' page https://www.lsbme.la.gov/content/statements-position, hosted on the board's Storyblok CDN). Key text: 'a physician's employment by a corporation other than a professional medical corporation is not per se unlawful' and the board 'state[s] only our position that a physician's employment by a business corporation does not per se violate the Medical Practice Act.' Caveat: a corporation itself cannot practice medicine, and an employer that controls the manner of care, termination, records access, or certain compensation terms may be treated as unlicensed practice. Some surveys list Louisiana as a 'CPOM state' with a no-interference exception, so EXCEPTIONS is a defensible alternative. Recent: CPOM statement updated Dec 2024 (originally 1992, reviewed 2001). LSBME also issued a 'Retail Intravenous (IV) Hydration Therapy' Statement of Position in Aug 2024, relevant to IV clinics (not reviewed in detail).",
  ME: "No primary source found online; the 1992 board advisory opinion is not posted. Secondary sources agree Maine has no CPOM doctrine and that the board holds physicians individually accountable regardless of employer ('company policy' is no defense). Recent changes (verified via Mintz summary and Maine Legislature PDF): (1) P.L. 2026, c. 690 (H.P. 1480), eff. Jan 1, 2027, requires 180-day advance notice to Maine DHHS and allows up to 270-day review (approve, condition, or disapprove) of acquisitions of majority interest or operational control of health care entities by private equity, hedge funds, or certain related MSOs. Small independent groups of 6 or fewer providers are excluded. (2) P.L. 2026, c. 661 (H.P. 1481), eff. July 14, 2026, requires copies of HSR filings to the AG. (3) P.L. 2026, c. 738 (L.D. 2233), approved Apr 16, 2026, merges the Board of Licensure in Medicine and Board of Osteopathic Licensure into a single 'Maine Board of Medicine' eff. Jan 1, 2027 (no CPOM provision found in that law). These do not create a CPOM ban but can affect investor-backed med spa deals.",
  MD: "Close call between STRICT and EXCEPTIONS. The Maryland Board of Physicians corporate FAQ (mbp.state.md.us/resource_information/faqs/resource_faqs_corporate.aspx) now says only that the Board 'does not give advice or information related to corporate issues' and points to Health Occ. § 14-302, so the older board statement (corporation may employ a physician only if it is a professional corporation, hospital, or HMO) is no longer posted. Gordon Feinblatt (Maryland firm, 2015) says the Board and AG have 'intimated' that LLCs owned by non-licensees may not practice medicine and that the doctrine's application is unclear. Survey on ndlegis.gov lists Maryland as CPOM 'Yes' with no nonprofit exception. Practical advice for med spas is usually PC plus MSO, so a reviewer may prefer STRICT. Recent: SB 494 / HB 944 (2026) would have created MHCC public-interest review of material change transactions including MSOs. The legislature site shows only committee hearings (Feb 2026) with no further action, so they appear not to have passed. MHCC released a PE-in-physician-practices report in Jan 2025 (PDF returned 403, not reviewed).",
  MA: "Verified 243 CMR 2.07(22) text: a licensee may practice through a professional corporation (c. 156A), nonprofit organizations (incl. c. 176A/176B corporations), an LLC under c. 156C or a partnership (with no liability-limiting provisions for the licensee's torts), or comparable out-of-state entities, and may be an employee of a licensed health care facility. General business corporations are excluded. Hooper Lundy (MA business lawyers article) confirms CPOM prohibition and cites c. 156C § 6 for professional LLCs. Why MEDIUM: (1) the licensed health care facility exception (e.g., DPH-licensed clinics) is a real route for non-physician ownership, so some reviewers may classify as EXCEPTIONS. (2) Whether a non-professional c. 156C LLC owned by non-physicians is allowed is debated. Recent: Chapter 343 of the Acts of 2024 (signed Jan 8, 2025, eff. Apr 8, 2025) expands Health Policy Commission material change notices to transactions with significant equity investors (private equity), and increases oversight of MSOs and REITs. An April 2026 AHLA presentation lists Massachusetts among states with further pending CPOM legislation (not verified as enacted).",
  MI: "Classification itself is well established. MEDIUM only because primary pages could not be fetched: ag.state.mi.us AG opinions returned 403 and legislature.mi.gov refused connections. Source used is a State Bar of Michigan Health Care Law Section article (2014, Hall Render author): CPOM prohibits a for-profit corporation or LLC from practicing medicine or employing a physician. Physicians (a 'learned profession') must organize as a PC or PLLC with all shareholders/members licensed. AG Op. 6770 (verified on vLex) lets nonprofit hospitals and other Nonprofit Corporation Act corporations employ physicians. Michigan State Medical Society letter to AG Nessel (Oct 23, 2023) confirms 'No Michigan law permits nonphysicians or for-profit entities owned by nonphysicians to own medical practices' and asks the AG to investigate PE/MSO 'physician in name only' structures. Recent: no enacted 2023 to 2026 CPOM/MSO statute found, but enforcement pressure on friendly-PC models is increasing.",
  MN: "Verified on vLex: the Minnesota Supreme Court affirmed the CPOM doctrine exists (relying on Granger v. Adson) and held corporate employment of chiropractors is prohibited except as expressly permitted by statute. The doctrine was held not to apply to massage or physical therapy. A later 2006 decision in the same case addressed remedies. Nonprofit exception rests on Minn. Op. Att'y Gen. 92-B-11 (1955). Hospitals and nonprofits (e.g., large systems) employ physicians. Professional firms must be owned by licensed professionals. Recent: Minn. Stat. ch. 145D (2023, HF 402) requires pre-closing notice of health care transactions (60 days to AG and MDH for entities with $80M+ revenue, 30 days to MDH for $10M to $80M) and lets the AG sue to block or unwind transactions. 2025 proposals would expand notice to PE/for-profit investments (status not verified).",
  MS: "Primary source verified (board policy compilation linked from https://www.msbml.ms.gov/board-policies, 'As last revised December 2025'). Policy 3.02: the Board will 'not concern itself with the form or type of business arrangements entered into by a licensee' provided the physician is MS-licensed, treatment is in the physician's 'sole and absolute discretion', billing and fee amounts are left to the physician 'whenever possible', there are no referral inducements, and anti-kickback laws are followed. The billing/fee condition is unusual and could matter for med spa pricing set by a lay owner. Flag for the reviewer. Old case law (e.g., Sears Roebuck v. State Bd. of Optometry, 1952) concerns optometry. Policy 3.15 lets trained NPs administer Botox under physician collaboration. Recent changes: none found.",
  MO: "No primary source states the rule clearly online. Multiple secondary sources agree: Martin Pringle (2022, verified) says 'general business entities in Missouri can hire or contract with physicians'. The law-firm survey on ndlegis.gov lists Missouri 'No' citing McKittrick v. Gate City Optical (1936) and Sager v. Lewin (1907). Permit Health agrees. Caution: the vLex summary of Sager v. Lewin was ambiguous about its holding, so it is not used as the sourceUrl. Some med spa marketing sites claim NPs/PAs cannot own med spas in Missouri. That appears to conflate ownership with collaborative practice and supervision rules (APRN collaborative practice arrangements under RSMo 334.104) and was not supported by any primary source. Recent: no 2023 to 2026 CPOM or MSO law found. APRN independence bills (e.g., SB 144 in 2025) were proposed. Enactment not verified.",
  MT: "Rule text verified (Cornell LII copy; same text reproduced in the Board's official MAR Notice 2026-269.1, published March 6, 2026 at https://dli.mt.gov/_docs/rules/2026-269pro-arm.pdf, which leaves subsection (m) unchanged). (m): unprofessional conduct to practice 'as the partner, agent, or employee of, or in joint venture with, a person who does not hold an equivalent license', except (i) professional service corporations, PLLCs, PLLPs, or (ii) with 'a licensed health care facility or other licensed health care provider' if there is a written agreement that the relationship will not affect independent judgment, judgment is in fact unaffected, and no required referrals. Statute prohibiting CPOM was repealed in 1995 (per 2021 survey filed with ND Legislature). Classified EXCEPTIONS rather than STRICT because the carve-out for 'other licensed health care provider' is broad and undefined: an NP- or RN-owned clinic may arguably qualify, while a purely lay-owned med spa would not. Reviewer should double check how the Board interprets 'licensed health care provider' (individual vs. entity). The official rules.mt.gov page did not render via fetch. Some surveys (Permit Health) label MT simply as 'Yes, CPOM exists'. No recent CPOM-specific legislation found.",
  NE: "No primary source fetched successfully. The 1905 Nebraska Supreme Court decisions (State Electro-Medical Inst. v. State and v. Platner) held corporations may contract to furnish medical services through licensed physicians. CourtListener page for Platner did not render text. Nebraska AG site (ago.nebraska.gov) failed DNS during research. Search snippets reference a Nebraska AG opinion saying non-physician corporations may provide services through employed physicians absent lay control of medical judgment, but opinion number could not be verified. 2021 law-firm survey filed with ND Legislature also lists NE as 'No CPOM'. CONFLICT: Discern.com claims NE 'actively enforces' CPOM and cites Noel v. Pathology Medical Services (Neb. 2025), but that case is a shareholder oppression dispute within a physician PC and is not a CPOM ruling, so Discern appears unreliable. Neb. Rev. Stat. 21-2216 only governs ownership of professional corporations (an optional entity form), not a ban on lay ownership of other entities. No recent changes found.",
  NV: 'No Nevada statute expressly prohibits CPOM. Doctrine rests on AG opinions interpreting NRS ch. 89 (all PC owners must be licensed), NRS 630 fee-splitting and aiding unlicensed practice provisions. AGO 2002-10: corporate practice permissible only through NRS 89 PCs, HMOs (NRS 695C), medical service corporations (NRS 695B) and similar insurance-regulated entities. Statutory carve-outs for county/public hospitals (NRS 450), nonprofit medical schools and research institutions. Hospital employment of physicians is contested: 2010 AG opinion described a longstanding practice of contracting rather than employment. 2023 AB 11 would have expressly barred hospital employment of physicians; passed both houses but vetoed by Gov. Lombardo (June 2023). Holland & Hart PDF verified (2021 webinar). Primary AG opinion PDFs on ag.nv.gov and leg.state.nv.us exhibits were blocked (403/rejected) so could not be verified directly, hence MEDIUM. Permit Health (Dec 2025) also rates NV as stricter with active Board enforcement.',
  NH: "McLane Middleton (NH firm), July 20, 2025: 'New Hampshire ... does not have an explicit prohibition on the corporate practice of medicine' and physicians may work as employees, members, consultants or contractors of health care organizations. If organized as a professional corporation or PLLC (RSA 294-A), owners must be licensed, but that entity form is optional. Search snippets say the former fee-splitting prohibition RSA 329:23 was repealed in 1996 (not independently verified). No primary source (statute, AG opinion, or Board statement) affirmatively stating the rule was found, so MEDIUM. 2021 survey filed with ND Legislature and Permit Health also list NH as no CPOM. No recent changes found.",
  NJ: "Rule text verified. Permitted forms: solo practice, partnership/PA/LLC composed solely of health care professionals licensed in the same or closely allied profession, or employment by a general business corporation only where it is DOH-licensed as an HMO, hospital, long/short-term care facility, ambulatory care facility or other health care facility, or in narrow settings (employee first-aid clinics, union/fraternal nonprofits, schools, insurers for QA). General business corporations may contract to provide services 'exclusively of a non-professional nature' (MSO model). Could arguably be EXCEPTIONS because of the licensed-facility route (e.g., licensed ambulatory care facility), but for a typical med spa or wellness clinic owner the rule is strict, and enforcement (including insurance fraud litigation like Allstate v. Northfield) is relatively active. Northfield case citation taken from 2021 survey, not independently fetched. No recent (2023-2026) NJ CPOM statute changes confirmed.",
  NM: "Verified on official NM State Records Center site. 16.10.1.13(B): 'A business entity formed pursuant to the laws of the state of New Mexico is authorized to provide healthcare services ... if the healthcare services are provided by or under the direction of persons who are duly licensed to engage in the practice of medicine.' Adopted 7/1/10, expressly retroactive as a clarification. No ownership requirement. Search results also cite N.M. AG Op. 87-39 (not independently verified). Other rules (delegation, supervision, NP/PA scope) still apply. No recent CPOM changes found.",
  NY: "Verified NYSED page: 'Business corporations cannot hire a licensee to provide professional services because the law neither authorizes such action nor provides an exemption'; only sole proprietors, professional partnerships, PCs, PLLPs and PLLCs owned by licensees may offer professional services; aiding unlicensed practice is a felony (Educ. Law 6512). Exceptions: Article 28 licensed facilities (hospitals, diagnostic and treatment centers), certain nonprofits (per 2021 survey). Insurer fraud cases (Andrew Carothers, M.D., P.C. v. Progressive, N.Y. 2019; State Farm v. Mallela) enforce fraudulent-incorporation doctrine. Recent change: Public Health Law Article 45-A (signed May 3, 2023, effective Aug 1, 2023) requires physician practices and MSOs to notify DOH at least 30 days before material transactions (https://www.health.ny.gov/facilities/material_transactions/), relevant to MSO deals. Later proposals to expand review were not verified.",
  NC: "Verified Board position statement: 'businesses practicing medicine in North Carolina must be owned in their entirety by persons holding active North Carolina licenses.' Exceptions: hospitals, HMOs, public health clinics, charitable nonprofits. Mixed-licensee ownership allowed only for combinations listed in N.C. Gen. Stat. 55B-14(c) (e.g., physicians with PAs/NPs and certain others), so NP/PA co-ownership with a physician may be possible. Board warns physicians/PAs working in non-compliant arrangements may be disciplined for aiding unlicensed practice. Recent change: statement amended September 2025 (adds expectations for hospital-owned practices on patient notice and continuity). NCMB is a .org but is the official state licensing board.",
  ND: "Verified AG letter text: 'North Dakota public policy prohibits a corporation, other than a professional corporation, from employing physicians to provide medical care.' It is an informal advisory letter ('general observation'), not a formal opinion or court ruling, and defers to local prosecutors. Verified current NDCC 43-17-42 (ndlegis.gov/cencode/t43c17.pdf): 'Notwithstanding any other provision of law', a licensed hospital, nonprofit entity, or charitable trust may employ a physician under a written contract protecting independent judgment, and is then not engaged in the practice of medicine. The carve-out implies for-profit lay corporations remain restricted. 2023 SB 2148 amended 43-17-42 (originally described as nonprofit/charitable trust employment for hyperbaric oxygen therapy). Current codified text reads broadly ('nonprofit entity'), so reviewer may want to confirm whether a 2025 amendment broadened it. Enforcement history against for-profit clinics is unclear, so EXCEPTIONS is a defensible alternative. Permit Health (Dec 2025) says ND 'unequivocally enforces' CPOM.",
  OH: "Verified Board statement: 'Ohio law does not prohibit an Ohio licensed physician from rendering medical services as an employee of a corporation or any other form of business entity' and 'The Ohio legislature has made it clear that the corporate practice of medicine doctrine no longer exists in Ohio.' Based on ORC 4731.226 (effective April 10, 1998). Older AG opinions (1952, 1962) and Bricker v. Buhl Optical (1936) predate the statute. Exception: pain management clinics must be owned by Ohio-licensed MDs/DOs (ORC 4729.552). codes.ohio.gov refused connection during research, so statute text verified only as quoted in the Board statement. Board's HTML position statement page returned 404, but the PDF loads. 2021 survey lists OH as 'Yes' citing 4731.226 as if it were a CPOM rule, which conflicts with the Board's own statement. No recent changes found.",
  OK: 'McAfee & Taft (Oklahoma firm, June 2017) verified: Oklahoma Allopathic and Osteopathic Acts contain no prohibition on employment of physicians by non-physician entities; hospitals and related institutions (including corporations organized for hospital or medical care purposes) may employ physicians under 59 O.S. 492; Professional Entity Act authorizes but does not mandate professional entities. Article notes allopathic fee-splitting prohibition still exists, which matters for percentage-based management fees. AG Op. 02-20 cited by 2021 survey filed with ND Legislature; could not fetch the opinion itself (oscn.net refused connection, Justia 403). Article does not cover optometry, which has separate rules. No recent changes found.',
  OR: 'SB 951 signed June 9, 2025. Noncompete/NDA/non-disparagement limits effective immediately. MSO restrictions apply Jan 1, 2026 to entities formed or arrangements made after June 9, 2025, and Jan 1, 2029 to pre-existing arrangements. Key rules: physicians must hold majority of voting shares and constitute majority of directors/officers of medical entities; MSO and its owners/employees cannot hold a majority interest, serve as directors/officers, or control hiring, clinical protocols, coding/billing, payer contracting, etc.; restrictions on stock transfer restriction agreements (friendly PC model) and dual roles. Exemptions include hospitals and hospital-affiliated clinics, PACE, certain behavioral health and telemedicine entities without Oregon physical presence. Holland & Knight (May 2026) notes NP-owned practices fall outside the CPOM law if no physicians or PAs are involved, which is relevant to NP-owned med spas but likely not to ones that employ a physician Medical Director (reviewer should double check how a contracted medical director is treated). HB 3410 (2025) amended SB 951. Litigation: Eugene Emergency Physicians v. PeaceHealth (D. Or., 2026) ended with PeaceHealth agreeing to end its ApolloMD arrangement. ORS 58.375 majority-physician ownership verified on oregon.public.law. Official oregonlegislature.gov pages refused connection during research, so primary SB 951 text not fetched. Classification and dates corroborated by Reed Smith, Holland & Knight, and multiple other firms.',
  PA: "No statute or Board statement found that expressly states the rule. Doctrine rests on Neill v. Gimbel Bros. (1938, an optometry case: a corporation may not employ licensed professionals to serve its customers). Pennsylvania firms (Lamb McErlane, SMGG Law 2022 'Medspas Must Comply With Pennsylvania's CPOM Doctrine', Stevens & Lee 2024) all advise that it applies to medical practices and med spas. Exceptions: hospitals, HMOs, licensed health care facilities, and professional entities owned by licensees (PCs, LLPs, restricted professional companies). Stevens & Lee cites Apollon v. OCA and OCA v. Hodges (orthodontics) invalidating MSAs with excessive MSO control. CONFLICT/UNCERTAINTY: a Duquesne Law student piece (April 2026) calls the doctrine 'merely symbolic: observed in form but not in practice', and some summaries describe PA as permitting corporate employment. Physician-specific enforcement is thinly documented, so EXCEPTIONS is a defensible alternative. 2021 survey filed with ND Legislature and Permit Health both list PA as 'Yes'. Pennsylvania health care transaction review bills (2024-2025) were not confirmed as enacted.",
  RI: "Verified via FindLaw: § 7-1.2-301 bars organizing a business corporation 'for the purpose of rendering the professional services specified in chapter 5.1', which must instead be organized as a PSC under ch. 7-5.1 (licensed shareholders/officers/directors). Holland & Knight (Feb 2026) says RI 'maintains restrictions on the corporate practice of several healthcare professions, including medicine' and allows PSCs and professional LLCs. AmSpa says RI currently restricts medical practice ownership to licensed providers. No AG opinion or case found that squarely applies the doctrine; enforcement history unclear, and treatment of lay-owned LLCs is not addressed by the statute I verified. 2021 law-firm survey (ndlegis.gov testimony) lists RI as CPOM 'Yes' with nonprofits allowed (RIH Medical Foundation v. Nolan, 723 A.2d 1123 (R.I. 1999)). RECENT: 2026 S 2459 / H 7721 'Rhode Island Ban on the Corporate Practice of Medicine Act' would codify CPOM, require physician majority voting control, ban straw ownership/dual PC-MSO interests, restrict MSOs and noncompetes, and add ownership reporting. H7721 was held for further study (Apr 9, 2026); no evidence it passed. rilegislature.gov was unreachable from my network, so bill status was confirmed only via AmSpa/Lengea/UrologyTimes. Reviewer: watch for reintroduction in 2027.",
  SC: "Source is the SC State Library archive copy of the Board's advisory opinion (page loads 200 via curl; WebFetch gets 403; metadata/abstract verified through the DSpace API). Abstract: Board 'does not license or regulate corporations' but expects the physician's professional judgment to be 'independently exercised, regardless of the economic relationship or business form involved', and licensees may not employ or permit unlicensed persons to practice medicine. Original llr.sc.gov PDF (https://www.llr.sc.gov/med/pdf/Laws/Corporate Practice of Medicine.pdf) was unreachable from my network. Baird v. Charleston County (1999) states SC has a common-law CPOM prohibition (via 2021 law-firm survey and secondary sources; case text not fetched). Classified EXCEPTIONS because the doctrine exists in case law but the Board's position is effectively permissive about business form, so it is weak in practice. Some secondary sources (med spa vendors) say SC requires a physician-controlled clinical entity, others say lay ownership is fine; conflicting. Could arguably be PERMISSIVE. Also note the 2023 joint position statement on retail IV therapy (llr.sc.gov/med/Policies/Joint-Position-Statement-Retail-IV-Therapy.pdf, not fetched). No 2023 to 2026 CPOM legislation found.",
  SD: "Verified statute text: public policy that a corporation may not practice medicine, but a corporation employing a physician is not practicing medicine if the agreement (1) does not supplant, diminish or regulate the physician's independent judgment, (2) does not result in profit to the corporation from the practice of medicine itself (corporation may not charge more for the physician's services than a reasonable independent physician fee, but may add facility/equipment/administrative charges), and (3) has an initial term of no more than three years with annual renewal thereafter. Enacted 1993, carve-out for ch. 47-11 professional corporations. Applies to for-profit and nonprofit corporations per 2021 law-firm survey. Contract drafting is important for med spa owners. No recent (2023 to 2026) CPOM changes found.",
  TN: "FindLaw copy (current as of Jan 2024) verified: § 63-6-204 permits specific entities to employ physicians (entity's own employees, community mental health centers, FQHCs/RHCs, hospitals with conditions, nursing homes via § 68-11-205), which presupposes a general CPOM prohibition, but the statute does not itself state the prohibition expressly. 2021 law-firm survey (ndlegis.gov) lists TN as CPOM 'Yes', no nonprofit exception. Several law-firm/secondary sources describe TN as a CPOM state. Exceptions are facility-type, not relevant to med spas, so STRICT for this audience (could also be labeled EXCEPTIONS). Med spa rule 0880-02-.24 (verified on Cornell LII): med spas offering cosmetic medical services must register with the Board of Medical Examiners and name a TN-licensed MD/DO supervising physician with an active TN practice who accepts responsibility; the rule has no explicit ownership clause. RECENT: 2025 HB 979 (signed May 21, 2025, eff. July 1, 2025) lets hospitals in counties of 105,000 or less employ radiologists, anesthesiologists, pathologists, and ER physicians (from ABA summary in search results; ABA page returned 403, not fetched). Justia and the TN SOS rules PDF were blocked (403).",
  TX: "TMB page (verified) describes nonprofit health centers as an 'exception to the general rule against corporate practice of medicine' and requires 162.001(b) organizations to be incorporated and directed by TMB-licensed physicians. TMB 'Additional Practice Topics' page (verified) notes Health & Safety Code § 311.061 hospital exceptions (counties under 50,000, critical access, sole community hospitals). AmSpa (2019) cites Occ. Code §§ 155.001, 164.052 (aiding/abetting unlicensed practice by a corporation), 165.152. Texas statutes site is a JS app and could not be text-verified, and the old TMB CPOM FAQ / Rule 177.17 page is gone (404), so reviewer may want to confirm whether 22 TAC 177.17 survived TMB's rule reorganization. Lay owners use the PA/PLLC + MSO model. RECENT: HB 3749 (89R, 'Jenifer's Law') relating to the regulation of elective intravenous therapy was signed June 20, 2025, effective Sept. 1, 2025 (verified on capitol.texas.gov). Secondary sources say it adds medical director/oversight and IV administration rules for IV therapy providers but does not change CPOM ownership rules. Original 2025 drafts touching med spa ownership were removed.",
  UT: "Verified (FindLaw, current as of Jan 1, 2025): a physician may practice as an employee of another person, or through a business corporation, LLC, PC, partnership, or any other form not prohibited by division rule, provided medicine is practiced only by Utah-licensed physicians. 2021 law-firm survey (ndlegis.gov) lists UT as no CPOM. le.utah.gov was unreachable from my network. A le.utah.gov PDF in search results is marked 'Superseded 5/7/2025', so the section was amended in 2025; reviewer should confirm the current text still permits employment by business corporations (secondary sources dated 2026 still describe Utah as permissive). Supervision and delegation rules still apply.",
  VT: "Classification rests on absence of a CPOM rule (2021 law-firm survey lists VT as 'No'). The House-passed H.583 contained an express CPOM ban (§ 9531, physician majority ownership, MSO limits), but the version passed by both chambers (verified PDF: https://legislature.vermont.gov/Documents/2026/Docs/BILLS/H-0583/H-0583%20As%20Passed%20by%20Both%20House%20and%20Senate%20Official.pdf) replaced it with 18 V.S.A. ch. 233 'Clinical Decision Making': private equity groups and hedge funds (and entities they control) may not interfere with providers' clinical judgment or control listed clinical/business decisions; health care facilities and MSOs must report PE/hedge fund ownership to the Green Mountain Care Board (by March 1, 2027) or attest none. Bill status page (verified) shows Act 133, Governor approved June 15, 2026, effective July 1, 2026. Some secondary sources (e.g., MedCentral snippet) describe the final law as a CPOM ban; the enacted text I reviewed does not contain the general ban. Reviewer should read the 'As Enacted' Act 133 PDF to confirm. A PE-backed med spa would be affected; an owner-operated one generally not. VT is also an NP full practice authority state.",
  VA: "Verified: § 54.1-111(F) says nothing in that section or §§ 13.1-543, 13.1-1102, 54.1-2902, 54.1-2929 prohibits an entity of a type listed in § 13.1-542.1 or 13.1-1101.1 that employs or contracts with a health-board licensee from practicing that profession through the licensee. Verified § 13.1-542.1 lists ordinary stock (ch. 9) and nonstock (ch. 10) corporations, not just PCs. 2021 law-firm survey lists VA as no CPOM (citing Board of Medicine Guidance Doc. 85-21, which is no longer on the Board's current guidance list). Secondary sources cite VA AG opinions supporting physician employment by corporations so long as physicians control medical judgment. No 2023 to 2026 CPOM changes found.",
  WA: "Verified (FindLaw opinion text): the court 'adhere[s] to the traditional understanding that the corporate practice of medicine doctrine forbids employment of health care professionals by business entities or nonprofessionals absent legislative authorization.' Exceptions: hospitals/hospital districts and entities with legislative authorization. Important nuance for RN/NP owners: verified RCW 18.100.050(5)(a) lets professionals licensed under listed chapters, including 18.71 (MD), 18.57 (DO), 18.71A (PA), and 18.79 (nursing, which covers ARNPs and RNs), co-own one professional service corporation. So nurse/NP co-ownership with physicians is possible, but lay ownership is not. ARNPs have independent practice in WA. RECENT: SB 5387 / 2SSB 5387 (2025 to 2026) would have codified CPOM and restricted MSO/PC structures. Per search results (Holland & Knight Feb 2026, LegiScan) it died in the Senate (Rules X file, Feb 26, 2026). Not independently verified on leg.wa.gov. Watch for reintroduction in 2027. Could arguably be EXCEPTIONS given the multi-profession PSC rule.",
  WV: "Verified (scanned PDF, read as image): 'The West Virginia Medical Practice Act incorporates explicit prohibitions upon the corporate practice of medicine'. Medical corporations need a Board certificate of authorization under § 30-3-15 and 'all shareholders of Medical Corporations must be licensed physicians or podiatrists'. Nominee/beneficial shareholder structures must be disclosed, and a non-licensed beneficial owner blocks authorization unless it is a nonprofit (e.g., nonprofit hospital) found not to violate CPOM. Courts recognize a nonprofit hospital contracting exception. Adopted Nov. 3, 2014, re-adopted Mar. 19, 2018. No 2023 to 2026 CPOM changes found.",
  WI: "Secondary source (O'Neil Cannon law firm, Mar. 2021, verified): WI CPOM 'is derived not only from the Wisconsin Medical Practices Act, but also from guidance established in Wisconsin Attorney General Opinions'. Licensed professionals may organize as service corporations or partnerships (LLC treatment 'less clear'), fee splitting with non-physicians is banned, and enforcement is weaker than in states like New York. Primary AG opinion (https://docs.legis.wisconsin.gov/document/oag/vol75-200) and State Bar paper (wisbar.org) were unreachable from my network. 2021 law-firm survey lists WI CPOM 'Yes' with a nonprofit medical education/research organization exception (Wis. Stat. § 448.05(5)). Search results note no recent enforcement history. Hospitals commonly employ physicians in WI. Classified EXCEPTIONS because the rule rests on old AG opinions and is weakly enforced. Some sources treat WI as effectively permissive. Reviewer may want to confirm with a Wisconsin primary source.",
  WY: "Secondary source only: 50-state law-firm survey submitted as testimony to the North Dakota Legislature (Jan. 2021, verified PDF) lists Wyoming CPOM 'No', citing Wyo. Stat. §§ 17-3-101 to 17-3-104 and Wyo. AG Op. 79-17. Could not locate or fetch the AG opinion text. Multiple 2026 secondary sources agree WY does not enforce CPOM. WY is an NP full practice authority state. No 2023 to 2026 CPOM changes found.",
}
